          %p
            There is an auxiliary concept of a
            %em pre-vector bundle
            over a pre-manifold which is also introduced. A pre-manifold defines a pre-vector bundle in a natural way, modelling the fact that the tangent bundle of an ordinary manifold is a vector bundle. The initial reason for introducing pre-vector bundles is that it can be shown that the total space of a pre-vector bundle is again a pre-manifold, and thus if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is a pre-manifold then we can define an associated pre-manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T M
            and so on.
          %p
            The next important concept is that of a
            = succeed "," do
              %em smooth map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{1}
            in the papers, is a truncated version.
          #defnb.num_defn.thdefn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              be pre-manifolds. A set map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi M
                    %mo →
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon M \to N
              is smooth if there is a sequence of maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mi n
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mi n
                    %mi M
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mi n
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^{n} f \colon T^{n} M \to T^{n} N
              with the property that for each
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mi n
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (T^{n} f)_{*} \colon \mathcal{S} (\mathbb{R},M) \to \mathcal{S} (\mathbb{R},N)
              makes sense and satisfies
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi T
                        %mi n
                      %mi f
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                      %msub
                        %mi δ
                        %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi δ
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi T
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %mi f
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (T^{n} f)_{*} \delta _{0} = \delta _{0} (T^{n-1} f)_{*}
          %p
            An
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{1}
            map is simply a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi M
                  %mo →
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon M \to N
            for which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{*} \colon \mathcal{S} (\mathbb{R},M) \to \mathcal{S} (\mathbb{R},N)
            is defined.
          #sectiond.num_section
          %h2#relationship_to_other_theories.num_section Relationship to Other Theories
          %p
            Underneath the structure of a pre-manifold is a notion of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalised+smooth+space"} generalized smooth space
            which fits in with the scheme defined in (
            %a{:href => "#MR2805746"}> Stacey 2011
            ). It is formed by taking the category of test spaces to be the one-object category associated to the monoid
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (\mathbb{R},\mathbb{R})
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Set
          #defnc.num_defn.thdefn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒦ℳ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{KM}
              be the category of generalised smooth spaces so described.
          %p
            The input forcing condition is extremely weak. This means that it sits far to the left in the diagram at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalised+smooth+spaces"} generalized smooth spaces
          %p
            In the literature, Chen’s first (1973) definition comes closest to this in terms of forcing condition but the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%B6licher+spaces"} Frölicher spaces
            is closest in terms of underlying category and test category.
          %p
            There is an inclusion functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ𝓇
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒦ℳ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Fr} \to \mathcal{KM}
            and this has a left adjoint which is found by saturating the smooth curves with respect to the smooth functions to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
          %p
            For
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chen+spaces"} Chen spaces
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒦ℳ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{KM}
            but it is no longer an inclusion (for example,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} ^{2}
            with the standard diffeology and with the wire diffeology give the same object in
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒦ℳ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{KM}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} ^{2}
            we recover the wire diffeology and not the standard one.
